course: geography
lesson: 14
title: Latin America and the Caribbean
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Latin America is named after a language, not a place: it is the part of the Americas where people mainly speak Spanish or Portuguese, languages that grew from Latin, and sometimes French.
explain: Why do Brazilians speak Portuguese while their neighbours speak Spanish? In 1494, in the Treaty of Tordesillas, Spain and Portugal drew a line 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde islands, and the eastern bulge of South America fell on Portugal's side.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5, colonising looks like filling empty provinces with settlers. Really the land was home to millions of people: after 1492 the Indigenous population collapsed, in many regions by perhaps nine out of ten within a century, mostly from new diseases such as smallpox, along with war and forced labour.
explain: Picture Mexico City today: its cathedral stands on the ruins of Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital on an island in a lake, which the Spanish conquered in 1521. The old lake bed is soft, so parts of the city are slowly sinking.
explain: The mountains shape everything here: Chile is squeezed into a long thin strip by the Andes, and Bolivia's government works in La Paz, about 3,600 metres up, where visitors gasp for breath.
fact: Latin America means the countries of the Americas where languages that grew from Latin are spoken, mainly Spanish and Portuguese, and sometimes French.
fact: Brazil is the largest country in South America, with about 213 million people, and its capital is Brasília, a planned city built far inland that replaced Rio de Janeiro in 1960, to bring people and growth into the inland, away from the coast.
fact: In 1494 Spain and Portugal agreed in the Treaty of Tordesillas to divide newly found lands along a line 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde islands, which gave Portugal the eastern bulge of South America.
fact: Mexico City was built on the ruins of Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital on an island in Lake Texcoco, which the Spanish conquered in 1521.
fact: After 1492 the Indigenous population of the Americas collapsed, in many regions by perhaps nine out of ten within a century, mostly from new diseases such as smallpox, along with war and forced labour; the exact numbers are still debated.
fact: Brazil received about 4.9 million enslaved Africans, more than any other country, and in 1888 it was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery.
fact: In the Haitian Revolution of 1791 to 1804, enslaved people overthrew French rule and founded Haiti, the first independent Black republic, with its capital at Port-au-Prince.
fact: Most of Spain's mainland American colonies won independence between 1810 and 1825, led by figures such as Simón Bolívar and José de San Martín, and Bolivia is named after Bolívar.
fact: Bolivia's official capital is Sucre, but its government sits in La Paz at about 3,600 metres, the highest seat of government in the world, where the air is thin, with less oxygen, so newcomers get short of breath, while Quito in Ecuador, at about 2,850 metres, is the highest official capital.
fact: Chile, with its capital Santiago, is about 4,300 km long but on average less than 200 km wide, squeezed between the Andes and the Pacific.
fact: The Caribbean islands were colonised by Spain, Britain, France, the Netherlands and Denmark, and Cuba, with its capital Havana, is the largest island; when Columbus arrived in 1492 the islands were home to peoples such as the Taíno.
fact: Lima, Peru's capital, was founded by the Spanish in 1535, while the Inca capital had been Cusco, high in the Andes.
exercise: Why is it called «Latin» America? | Most people there speak languages that grew from Latin | The Romans once ruled it, long before the Spanish came | Its people speak Latin at home | It was discovered by Italians, and Italian comes from Latin
exercise: What is the capital of Brazil? | Brasília | Rio de Janeiro | São Paulo | Buenos Aires
exercise: When did Brasília replace Rio de Janeiro as Brazil's capital? | In 1960 | In 1500 | In 1822 | In 2000
exercise: Why do Brazilians speak Portuguese? | The Treaty of Tordesillas gave Portugal the eastern bulge of South America | Portugal bought Brazil from Spain in 1900 | Portuguese sailors were the only Europeans who could cross the Atlantic in those days | Brazil's Indigenous people already spoke Portuguese
exercise: What city stood where Mexico City is today? | Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital | Cusco, the old Inca capital in the mountains | Machu Picchu | Havana
exercise: What mostly caused the Indigenous population of the Americas to collapse after 1492? | New diseases such as smallpox, along with war and forced labour | A great flood that covered both continents for many years after 1492 | Most people sailed away to Europe | A long cold winter
exercise: Which country received the most enslaved Africans and was the last in the Americas to end slavery? | Brazil, in 1888 | The United States, in 1865 | Cuba, in 1808 | Mexico, in 1950
exercise: Which country was founded by enslaved people who overthrew French rule? | Haiti | Cuba, after a revolt | Jamaica | Brazil
exercise: Which leader helped free much of South America and gave his name to a country? | Simón Bolívar | Christopher Columbus | Hernán Cortés | Pedro Álvares Cabral
exercise: In which years did most of Spain's mainland American colonies win independence? | Between 1810 and 1825 | Between 1492 and 1500 | Between 1900 and 1950 | Between 1700 and 1710
exercise: Where does Bolivia's government sit? | In La Paz, the highest seat of government in the world | In Sucre, a busy port by the sea on Bolivia's long Pacific coast | In Lima | In Quito
exercise: Which is the highest official capital in the world? | Quito, in Ecuador | La Paz, in Bolivia | Mexico City, in Mexico | Bogotá, in Colombia
exercise: What gives Chile its long, thin shape? | It is squeezed between the Andes and the Pacific | It was cut out of Argentina with a ruler | A huge river runs along its whole length from north to south | The Atlantic Ocean wore it away
exercise: Which is the largest Caribbean island? | Cuba | Jamaica | Puerto Rico | Barbados
exercise: Which country did NOT colonise Caribbean islands? | Russia | Denmark | The Netherlands | Great Britain
exercise: What was the capital of the Inca Empire? | Cusco | Lima | Quito | Tenochtitlan
test: Sam reckons Brazilians speak Spanish «coz it's South America innit». What is right? | Brazilians speak Portuguese, because Brazil was a Portuguese colony | Brazilians speak Spanish like their neighbours, with a few Portuguese words | Brazilians speak Latin | Brazilians speak French
test: In Europa Universalis 5, a colony grows in an «empty» province in the Americas. What is wrong with that picture? | The land was already home to Indigenous peoples whose societies colonisation broke | Nothing, the Americas had no people | The provinces were empty because the Ice Age had only just ended there | Only Europeans could live in the Americas
test: «capital of brazil? rio. duh.» What is right? | It has been Brasília since 1960; Rio was the capital before that | It is still Rio de Janeiro, as it has been since the Portuguese king came | It is São Paulo | It is Buenos Aires
test: What would happen if a visitor flew straight from the beach to La Paz and ran up a hill? | They could feel short of breath, because the air holds less oxygen up high | They would float a little, because the air up there is so much lighter | Nothing, the air is the same everywhere | They would get warmer and warmer the higher up they went, as they near the Sun
test: «why's mexico city sinking, is it on the sea??» Which is the best answer? | It is built on the soft bed of an old lake, and the ground is slowly sinking | It is floating on the Pacific Ocean | A volcano under the city is slowly sucking it down into the earth | It is not sinking at all
test: Why was the Haitian Revolution so important in world history? | Enslaved people won their freedom and founded the first independent Black republic | It made Haiti a French colony again | It was the first war fought with ships | It ended slavery everywhere at once, in every country of the world, in the same year
test: «so chile is like 4000 km long and like 5 km wide lol» What is right? | It is about 4,300 km long and on average less than 200 km wide | It is about 4,300 km wide and only 5 km long, like a line | It is perfectly round | It is 200 km long and 4,300 km wide
test: A cartoon shows Columbus landing on an empty island with nobody there. What is wrong? | The islands were full of people, such as the Taíno, who already lived there | Nothing, the islands were empty | Columbus landed in Australia | Columbus never sailed at all, and the islands were found by the Vikings
test: «bolivia's capital is sucre — no wait, la paz? which» Which is the best answer? | Sucre is the official capital and La Paz is where the government sits | Only La Paz is a capital | Only Santa Cruz is a capital, as it is the biggest city | Bolivia has no capital
test: In 1494, who decided which European country got which lands in the Americas? | Spain and Portugal, in the Treaty of Tordesillas | The Aztec emperor | The people living in the Americas | Britain and France, in a treaty signed on a ship at sea
test: «why'd they build brasília in the middle of nowhere» Which is the best answer? | To bring people and growth into the inland of the country, away from the coast | Because Rio sank into the sea | Because the Portuguese king lived there | Because the Amazon flooded every coastal city in Brazil, so they had to move inland
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the Portuguese language is spoken in the Federative Republic of Brazil. | Under the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494, eastern South America fell to Portugal, which colonised it | Brazil was purchased from Spain by Portugal in 1900 | The Indigenous peoples of Brazil spoke Portuguese prior to 1492 | Portuguese was adopted by decree in 1960 upon the founding of Brasília
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capitals of the Plurinational State of Bolivia? | Sucre is the constitutional capital, whereas the seat of government is situated in La Paz | La Paz is the sole capital in respect of all functions | Sucre is the seat of government, whereas La Paz is a coastal port | Bolivia possesses no designated capital
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the population of the Americas subsequent to the year 1492. | The Indigenous population declined catastrophically, chiefly owing to introduced diseases, warfare and forced labour | The Indigenous population increased substantially owing to European trade | The Indigenous population remained unchanged | The Indigenous population emigrated voluntarily to Europe
exam: State the year in which the Federative Republic of Brazil abolished slavery. | 1888 | 1865 | 1822 | 1960
